// D0161 — PROPOSED. `.knowledge/` as a keel-structured store for the graph D0153's process produces.
// TWO STATED INVARIANTS ARE BROKEN ON PURPOSE and named as scoped exceptions rather than glossed:
// the two-model rule (§1 intro) and UUID identity (§1.3). If either exception is unacceptable, the design
// does not survive and should be rejected outright rather than trimmed.
// #ProspectiveChange (D0070): adds a top-level structure and a schema; derives no requirement yet.
package Decision0161 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineWork::*;
    private import EngineVerification::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    #ProspectiveChange part d0161 : Decision {
        :>> id = "b8407e15-3c69-4d2a-9016-5ea7f3c04b9d";
        :>> title = "D0161: `.knowledge/` — a keel-structured knowledge store; computed identity, one file per fact shape, question-coverage as the gate";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-18";
        :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5";
        :>> status = DecisionStatus::proposed;
        :>> context = "D0153 accepted the knowledge-graph-memory process — eight sequenced steps, questions first, a closed vocabulary the human owns, extraction into typed entities, relations and aliases, then seeding plus traversal, injection, a trap test and recorded limits. ITS STEP 3 SPECIFIES A STORE AND NAMES NO LOCATION: the process says 'the store schema — one table per fact shape' and describes tiers up to a server graph database, and nothing in the repository says where a tier-1 store lives or what format it takes. So the accepted process emits entities, relations and aliases with nowhere to put them, which is the gap this Decision fills — the demand is rooted in an accepted Decision rather than in a new idea. MEASURED CONSTRAINT that shapes the answer: this repository's .tracking model holds roughly 8000 items in 369 SysML files, and /api/decisions was observed today taking 12.8 and then 33.2 seconds while every other endpoint answered in under 150ms. A machine-extracted fact corpus is larger than the work model by construction, so the storage format is a load-bearing choice and not a preference.";
        :>> decision = "PROPOSED: a top-level `.knowledge/` directory, structured like keel and gated separately. LAYOUT — `.knowledge/questions/<set>.sysml` (HUMAN-OWNED: the questions the graph must answer, D0153 step 1, declared as items so they can be counted and traced); `.knowledge/schema/vocabulary.sysml` (HUMAN-OWNED: the closed vocabulary — entity types, relation types, and one declared FactShape per shape); `.knowledge/facts/<shape>.jsonl` (MACHINE-WRITTEN: one file per fact shape, one fact per line — entities.jsonl, relations.jsonl, aliases.jsonl, and a NEW FILE for any new shape, never a nullable column in an existing one); `.knowledge/sources/sources.jsonl` (one line per ingested document with its content hash). IDENTITY IS COMPUTED, not minted: a fact's id is a hash of its type and normalised name, per D0153 step 4, so re-extraction MERGES instead of duplicating. PROVENANCE IS TWO-AXIS: every fact line carries its extractor and extraction time (an actor, as everywhere in keel) AND its source id plus the source's content hash at extraction — so a fact whose document has since changed is computably SUSPECT, reusing the judgedAgainst-drift mechanism on a different subject. COMPUTED VIEWS, never authored: question-coverage (for each declared question, can the graph answer it — seeding hits an entity and traversal reaches an answer), suspect (facts whose source hash moved), orphans (entities with no relation; facts whose source does not resolve), neighbourhood (the existing configurable slice, over knowledge edges), and vocabulary-drift (any fact using a type absent from the closed vocabulary). GATING IS A SEPARATE LANE: the work-discipline guards MUST NOT run over `.knowledge/` — charter, ceremony, sprint-coverage, retro-backlog and the rest are meaningless for a fact corpus — and duplicate-identity must be EXCLUDED because here a duplicate is the merge mechanism rather than a defect. What gates instead: vocabulary conformance, source resolvability, and fact-shape conformance.";
        :>> rationale = "WHY A THIRD MODEL AT ALL, which breaks the two-model rule stated in CLAUDE.md's opening: the engine model tracks the WORK and the deliverable is what the work PRODUCES, and domain knowledge is neither — it is an INPUT consumed while working. Filing it under .tracking would make the work model contain facts about the world, which is precisely the conflation §1 forbids in the other direction; filing it under the deliverable would make it a product nobody ships. So the honest options are a third model or no store, and D0153 already accepted the process that needs one. THE COST IS CONCRETE AND MUST BE PAID IN THE SAME CHANGE: /api/scope computes engine | deliverable | UNSCOPED and would need a third classification, and CLAUDE.md's two-model sentence would need revising — doc-sync rides it. WHY COMPUTED IDENTITY, which breaks §1.3's immutable UUID: in the work model identity is minted from a CSPRNG so two items never collide, because each item is a distinct act. In a knowledge model the same referent is extracted from many documents and must converge on ONE node with no matching service, which is only true if the id is derived from the content. D0153 fixed this already ('identity is computed, never looked up ... the hash is why re-runs MERGE instead of duplicating'), so the exception is inherited rather than invented — but it is a real exception to a stated invariant and it is why `.knowledge/` needs its own schema rather than importing schema/core. WHY JSONL FOR FACTS AND SYSML FOR VOCABULARY: the paper's model is one table per fact shape, and JSONL is the line-oriented form of a table — one line per fact makes each fact atomic, diffable and independently mergeable in git, which matters because extraction is machine-written at volume and re-runs touch many facts. SysML would inherit the parse cost already measured at 8000 items, and it buys nothing for a corpus with no owners, no gates and no ceremony. The VOCABULARY stays SysML because it is small, human-owned governance, and the existing marker and attribute-vocabulary guards can then see it — the same reasoning D0105 used to reject TOML for rules while keeping it for pure execution parameters. WHY QUESTION-COVERAGE IS THE HEADLINE VIEW: D0153 makes questions drive everything, and a graph is only as good as the questions it answers. Question-coverage is falsifiable in the way `keel verification` is — it can report that the graph CANNOT answer a declared question — which is the one number that would keep this store honest rather than merely large.";
        :>> consequences = "IF ACCEPTED: a third top-level model exists; /api/scope gains a classification; CLAUDE.md's two-model statement is revised in the same commit; a `keel knowledge <view>` command surface appears; and the guard lane must be explicitly bounded so the 35 work guards do not fire on a fact corpus. THE FIRST HONEST OBSERVATION: for THIS repository the store would likely start EMPTY — keel's own domain knowledge is its docs and decisions, so the first real user is a downstream project with documents to ingest. That is a reason to keep the design cheap, not a reason to skip it, because the accepted process is otherwise unimplementable. EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE: recall injection at the harness prompt-submit path (D0153 step 6) touches the agent harness rather than the store; tiers 2 and 3 (sqlite, a graph database) are deferred and the design must not foreclose them — JSONL loads into either. IF REJECTED: D0153's process stays accepted and unimplementable, and that should be recorded against the process rather than left as a silent gap, because an accepted process with no store is exactly the kind of inert clause the Decision passes kept finding.";
    }
}
